{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And every wise-hearted man among the doers of the work made the Tabernacle ten curtains.</b> Behold, the Torah repeats the work of the Tabernacle five times, for it mentioned all of it in the command both in detail and in general. First (above, 25:10–28:43) it said, \"And you shall make thus\" and \"And you shall make thus,\" and afterward (above, 31:7–11) it mentioned them all in a general way: \"And they shall make all that I have commanded you, the Tent of Meeting and the Ark of the Testimony,\" until \"according to all that I have commanded you, they shall make.\"<br>And the reason for this is that HaShem commanded Moses to tell Bezalel and Oholiab and all the wise men the work in its totality, and afterward they would draw near to it to make it, for they would not be fit for the sacred work until they heard all the work and understood it and undertook upon themselves that they would know how to complete it. And at the time of the execution (above, 35:11–20) it mentioned it first in the aforementioned general way, and said (above, 31:6–11), \"They shall come and make all that YHWH has commanded, the Tabernacle, its tent,\" etc. — and one detail is lacking from here, for Moses certainly needed to say to the wise men who do the work: Make the Tabernacle ten curtains; the length of a curtain is thus and the width thus, and so with all the work — but Scripture did not mention this, since it is known that he told them everything in order, after they did every single thing accordingly.<br>And the reason for this is that Moses did not need to spell out for them every act of the work item by item as was mentioned in the command (25:10–28:43) and in the execution (36:8–39:42), but rather he abbreviated for them as though he said: they shall make a Tabernacle of ten curtains, five opposite five, and they understood that they would make loops corresponding to one another and clasps of gold. And similar to this in all the work — in brief he hinted to them the matter, and they understood everything. Therefore he did not elaborate in this detail; Scripture alludes to their wisdom and understanding and the quality of insight that was in them. And afterward (in verses 36:8, 39:42) it repeated all the work in the manner of the first detail (above, 25:10–28:43): \"And every wise-hearted man among the doers of the work made the Tabernacle,\" \"And he made curtains of goat hair,\" \"And he made the boards,\" etc. And it would have been sufficient for the entire matter (above, 35:6–20) to say, \"And Moses said to all the congregation of the children of Israel: all the work that YHWH commanded him,\" and to say (here), \"And the children of Israel did according to all that YHWH commanded Moses, so they did. And Moses saw all the work, and behold, they had done it as YHWH had commanded, so they did it, and Moses blessed them\" (Exodus 39:43). But Scripture wished to say that Moses mentioned to all the congregation of the children of Israel and the wise men who were there (above, 35:11–20) all the work in general, for his intention in this was that the people would donate what was needed for the service of the great work, and so that the wise men would hear and know whether they would undertake to make it as they were commanded. And afterward it mentioned the execution in detail, to tell that all the wise men made the Tabernacle — this is what is stated (here): <b>And every wise-hearted man among the doers of the work made the Tabernacle ten curtains.</b><br>And it says, \"And he made curtains of goat hair\" (Exodus 36:14), \"And he made the boards\" (Exodus 36:20) — it means to say: and every wise-hearted man made curtains of goat hair. And concerning the Ark it says, \"And Bezalel made\" (Exodus 37:1), to tell that the great craftsman among them is the one who made the Ark alone. And the reason is because he was filled with the spirit of Elohim, in wisdom, in understanding, and in knowledge (Exodus 31:3), so that he would contemplate it and make it with the intended design that is desired, for there is no great craftsmanship in its making, but there are works there that require greater craftsmanship than it. And it returned and said simply, \"And he made the Table\" (Exodus 37:10), \"And he made the Menorah\" (v. 17).<br>According to the opinion of Rabbi Abraham (ibn Ezra, ad loc.), it alludes to Bezalel, that he made all the sacred vessels. But this is not my opinion, for concerning the court of the Tabernacle as well it says thus (Exodus 38:9). Rather, it refers back to all the wise-hearted men, as it said (here) concerning the Tabernacle. And after he completed the general and the particular in the execution, it mentioned the bringing to Moses in a general way, and said, \"And they brought the Tabernacle to Moses, the tent and all its vessels,\" etc. (Exodus 39:33), to tell of their wisdom, that they brought everything to him in order, for none of them brought his work before him until all the work was completed, as it says (in the verse before it), \"And all the work of the Tabernacle of the Tent of Meeting was completed.\" And after it was completed, they all gathered and showed it to him in order. They said first: Our master, here is the tent and here are its vessels, and afterward: here is the Ark and here are its poles, and so with everything.<br>Now, concerning the Tabernacle and all its vessels it did not mention \"as YHWH commanded Moses,\" but regarding these particulars in the priestly garments it does mention thus for each and every item (below, 39:1, 5, 7, 21, 26, 29, 31). And the reason is that it subsumed all the work of the Tabernacle in the verse it said at the end (Exodus 38:22): \"And Bezalel son of Uri son of Hur made all that YHWH commanded Moses.\" And perhaps this was because of the change of order, as our Sages mentioned (Berakhot 55a). And all this in general is a way of endearment and a way of distinction, to say that HaShem desired the work and mentions it in His Torah many times, to multiply the reward of those who engage in it, in the manner of what they said in the midrash: fine is the talk of the servants of the patriarchs before the Holy One, blessed be He, more than the Torah of the sons — for the section of Eliezer is two or three pages."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 36, :notes {0 "The parenthetical verse citations throughout are drawn from the Hebrew source's own bracketed references; the abbreviation שם א refers to Ibn Ezra's comment on Exodus 37:1. The closing midrash about Eliezer refers to Genesis 24."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:22:32.754569349Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}